{
  "dom_id": "step-q276366",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\u223cN(-2,{5}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<9).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<9)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-2$ and $\\sigma =5.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & =P(Z<\\frac{9-(-2)}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.2) \\\\  & =\u03a6(2.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/8ac4c36c58d5189e2483a71e2b0dbbe8e629dd9537d90b2338000d36915880a1.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\u03a6(2.2)=0.9861.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<9)=0.9861.\n$$",
